"""Grade 5 life essentials: friends, growing up and choosing well."""
from spec import L, U

E5 = U('Growing up well', 'Morality', 'How do I be a good friend, care for the body God gave me, and choose well?', [
L('Friends: being one, choosing them, and including the one who is left out',
  'Fifth graders are learning friendship the hard way: cliques, secrets, “you can’t sit here,” and a new pressure to fit in. The Catechism’s teaching on charity, friendship and the Golden Rule, together with the Gospel stories of the Good Samaritan and Zacchaeus, gives children four marks of a real friend and a concrete way to include others.',
  '1822-1829,2347,1939-1942,2477-2479,1878-1879,2447',
  'Proverbs 17:17;Luke 10:25-37;Luke 6:31;John 15:12-15',
  'charity solidarity dignity virtue',
  'What is one thing a good friend does? What is one thing a good friend never does?',
  'Fill out a “four marks” chart: A friend wants my good; tells me the truth kindly; is faithful; includes others. Write one example from your life for each, or one you wish for. Then write the name of a person who is often left out and one thing you will do for them this week.',
  'st-francis',
  id='5-L1',
  hook='Read the Good Samaritan in a minute. Ask: “Who passed by? Who stopped? What did stopping cost?” Then ask: “In our class or school, who often gets passed by?” Do not name names; just let children think.',
  teach=[
    'The Catechism says we are made for one another: “Man is a social being” (CCC 1879). Friendship is “a great good for all” (CCC 2347). Jesus said, “No longer do I call you servants... but I have called you friends” (John 15:15). The deepest friendship is charity: loving someone for their own sake, not for what they give you (CCC 1822–1823). A real friend wants your good.',
    'Four marks of a true friend. A true friend wants your good, and is glad when good things happen to you. A true friend tells you the truth kindly: “Faithful are the wounds of a friend” (Proverbs 27:6). A true friend is faithful, there when you are sad or when you make a mistake: “A friend loves at all times” (Proverbs 17:17). And a true friend includes others, and doesn’t use friendship as a weapon. If someone says “do this or I won’t be your friend,” that is pressure and not friendship.',
    'Jesus’ story of the Good Samaritan answers the question “Who is my neighbor?” (Luke 10:25–37). A traveler is hurt and two religious men walk by; a Samaritan, a stranger, stops and helps, paying for the man’s care. Jesus says, “Go and do likewise.” The Church calls it solidarity: we are responsible for one another (CCC 1939–1942). At school, that can be simple. Sit with the person who sits alone. Say their name. Invite them into the game.',
    'Be careful with words. The Catechism says gossip, spreading rumours and sharing secrets hurt a person’s good name (CCC 2477–2479). If a friend tells you a secret, keep it, unless it means someone could be hurt; then you must tell a trusted adult. If you hurt a friend, say sorry. If you are being bullied or left out, tell a grown-up. A good friendship is something we build, and sometimes repair.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Which of the four marks do you find easiest to show? Hardest?',
    'Why do some people leave others out? What could help?',
    'What do you do when a friend asks you to do something you know is wrong?',
    'Who has been a Good Samaritan to you?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Catechism say friendship is?|A waste of time|A great good for all|Only for adults|A reward|1|CCC 2347.',
    'What are four marks of a real friend?|Fun, popular, rich, clever|Wants your good, tells the truth kindly, is faithful, includes others|Always agrees, never argues|Does what you want|1|Proverbs 17:17; 27:6.',
    'What does the Good Samaritan teach?|To avoid strangers|We are neighbors to anyone who needs help|Only help friends|Only help the rich|1|Luke 10:25–37.',
  ],
  life='This week, pick one person who is often alone. Do one specific thing: sit with them at lunch, say hello by name, invite them to play. Do it again the next day. And choose one thing to be a better friend yourself: listen, say sorry, or keep a promise.',
  saint='Philip Neri: a joyful priest in Rome who made friends with street boys, played games with them, and taught them about God.',
  misc=[
    '“A real friend always agrees with me.”',
    '“Including someone new will cost me my friends.”',
    '“Keeping every secret is always right.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:aug-confessions'],
  mem='“A friend loves at all times.” (Proverbs 17:17)',
  assess='Ask each child to name the four marks of a friend, give an example, and say who they will include this week.',
  script=dict(
    open='Retell the Good Samaritan in a minute, with children acting: a traveler, two who pass, one who stops. Ask, “Who passed by? Who stopped? What did stopping cost?” Then say, quietly, “Who gets passed by at our school?”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “God made us for each other. The Church says friendship is a great good. Jesus said, ‘I have called you friends.’ The best friendship is love that wants what is good for the other person, not just what they can give you. So what are the marks of a real friend?”',
      'Say: “Four marks. One: a friend wants your good and is glad when good things happen to you. Two: a friend tells you the truth kindly. Proverbs says the wounds of a friend are faithful. Three: a friend is faithful, there when you are sad or when you make a mistake. Four: a friend includes others and doesn’t use friendship as a weapon. If someone says ‘do this or I won’t be your friend,’ that is pressure, not friendship.”',
      'Say: “Jesus told the story of the Good Samaritan to answer ‘Who is my neighbor?’ A hurt man, two who pass by, and a stranger who stops and pays for his care. Jesus says, ‘Go and do likewise.’ At school, that’s small. Sit with the one who sits alone. Say their name. Invite them into the game. The Church calls this solidarity: we are responsible for each other.”',
      'Say: “Careful with words. Gossip and rumors hurt. Keep a friend’s secret, unless someone could be hurt, then tell a trusted grown-up. If you hurt a friend, say sorry. If you are being bullied or left out, tell a grown-up. Friendship is something we build, and sometimes repair.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Who is a friend who has all four marks? How do you know?',
      'What makes it hard to include someone new?',
      'What is one thing you could do this week to be a better friend?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give out a four-marks chart. Write an example of each mark from your life.',
      'Write the name of someone who is often left out, and one thing you will do.',
      'In pairs, act out inviting someone to join a game.',
    ],
    close='Pray the prayer of St. Francis together. Say, “Be the friend you hope to find.”'),
  tryit=['Fill in the four marks.', 'Name one person to include.', 'Practise an invitation.'],
  saintStory='Philip Neri was born in Florence, Italy, in 1515. At eighteen he went to Rome to study, but spent most of his time praying in the catacombs, and caring for the poor and sick. He did not have much money, and lived in a small room, but he had a gift for friendship.\nHe loved to talk with young people, and he found them wherever they were: in the piazzas, markets and shops of Rome. He would stop and ask, “Well, brothers, when shall we begin to do good?” He took boys on picnics and pilgrimages, played games with them, and told jokes. In the evenings he would gather them for prayer, singing and a short talk. These meetings became the Oratory, which still exists.\nHe was famous for his humor. When a young man was too proud, he made him carry a small dog around, or shaved half his beard, to teach him humility. He was a priest by 1551, and heard confessions from morning to night. He died in 1595 at the age of eighty. He was canonized in 1622.',
),

L('My body is a gift: growing up, respect and modesty',
  'At ten and eleven, children begin to notice their changing bodies and to hear confused messages from peers and screens. Parents are the first teachers in this matter (CCC 2223), and the lesson is designed to support them: it uses plain, calm language about the body as a gift, privacy and respect, safe and unsafe touch, and trusted adults, with the Church’s positive teaching on dignity and purity. Teachers should send home a note before the lesson.',
  '364-365,2332-2333,2337-2338,2521-2524,2223,1700-1702,2354',
  'Psalm 139:13-16;1 Corinthians 6:19-20;Genesis 1:27,31;Luke 2:52',
  'dignity chastity virtue image-of-god',
  'What do you like about how God made you? What is something that is changing or confusing?',
  'Draw a simple “gift chart”: three things your body does that you are thankful for (running, hugging, singing). Then fill in the “safe, kind and private” rules: what is private (parts covered by a swimsuit), who may touch (parents or doctors, only to help, with permission), what to do if anything makes you uncomfortable (say no, go away, tell a trusted adult). Name three trusted adults.',
  'guardian-angel',
  id='5-L2',
  hook='Hold a wrapped gift. Ask: “If someone gave you a gift, how would you treat it? Would you throw it around or take care of it?” Then say, “Your body is a gift from God, and you are the one who takes care of it.”',
  teach=[
    'God made your body, and he said it was very good (Genesis 1:31). The Church teaches that you are a unity of body and soul: your body is not a costume, it is part of who you are (CCC 364–365). Psalm 139 says, “I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made.” Your body is a gift, and so is each person’s. You are made in God’s image, and so is everyone you meet (CCC 1700–1702).',
    'Growing up brings changes, and they are normal. Boys and girls are different in ways God designed, and each of us is to accept and be thankful for who we are (CCC 2333). Changes in size, voice, skin and feelings are part of growing. Your parents are the best people to talk to about them (CCC 2223), and they were once your age. If you have questions, it is good to ask a trusted adult, and not to rely on rumors or the internet.',
    'Modesty and privacy are ways of showing respect. The Catechism says modesty “protects the intimate center of the person” and “inspires a way of life which makes it possible to resist the allurements of fashion” (CCC 2521–2524). It means some things are private. Parts of the body covered by a swimsuit are private. Another person should not look at or touch those parts, except a parent or doctor helping with health, with permission. You should not look at or share private pictures of anyone (CCC 2354).',
    'Safety rules. You have the right to say “No” to any touch or secret that makes you uncomfortable. If anyone tries to touch you in a private way, asks you to keep a secret about touch or pictures, or shows you things that frighten you, say no, get away, and tell a trusted adult right away, and keep telling until someone helps. It is never your fault. That is exactly what God would want you to do. Chastity, which the Church describes as respect for the body in the service of love, begins here (CCC 2337–2338).',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is one thing about your body that you are thankful for?',
    'Why do you think some parts of the body are private?',
    'Who are the trusted adults you could ask or tell?',
    'Why is it never your fault if someone makes you feel unsafe?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does the Church teach about the body?|It is bad|It is a gift from God and part of who we are|It is a costume|It does not matter|1|CCC 364–365.',
    'Who are the first teachers in growing up, according to the Church?|Friends|Parents|The internet|Nobody|1|CCC 2223.',
    'What should you do if anything makes you feel unsafe?|Keep it secret|Say no, get away, and tell a trusted adult|Wait|Ask friends|1|CCC 2223, 2521.',
  ],
  life='Talk to a parent or trusted adult this week about one question you have. Also write the names of three trusted adults on a card and keep it somewhere safe. And do one thing to take care of your body: sleep, sports, healthy food, or a walk.',
  saint='Agnes: a young Roman girl who loved Jesus above everything and, according to tradition, would not give up her faith, even when she was in danger.',
  misc=[
    '“Talking about the body is always embarrassing or wrong.”',
    '“It is my fault if something bad happens.”',
    '“Secrets about touch are okay to keep.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:chastity'],
  mem='“I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made.” (Psalm 139:14)',
  assess='Ask each child to say that the body is a gift, name three trusted adults, and say what to do if anything feels unsafe.',
  script=dict(
    open='Send a note home beforehand. Show a wrapped gift. Ask, “If someone gave you this, how would you treat it?” Let children answer. Say, “Your body is a gift from God. You and your parents take care of it.” Keep your tone calm and matter-of-fact.',
    teach=[
      'Say: “God made your body and said it was very good. The Church teaches that you are body and soul together. Your body is part of who you are, not a costume. Psalm 139 says you are fearfully and wonderfully made. So is everyone you meet. We treat each person with respect because they are made in God’s image.”',
      'Say: “Growing up brings changes. They are normal. Your voice, your height, your skin, your feelings. Your parents are your best teachers about this; they were your age once. If you have questions, ask a trusted adult. Do not rely on rumors or the internet, which can be wrong or upsetting.”',
      'Say: “Modesty and privacy are ways of showing respect. Some things are private. The parts of the body covered by a swimsuit are private. Other people should not look at or touch them, except a parent or doctor helping with health, with permission. And we never look at or share private pictures of anyone. That protects people.”',
      'Say: “Safety rules. You can always say no to a touch or a secret that makes you uncomfortable. If anyone tries to touch you in a private way, or asks you to keep a secret about touch or pictures, or shows you things that frighten you: say no, get away, and tell a trusted adult right away. Keep telling until someone helps. It is never your fault.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What are you thankful for about your body?',
      'Who are three adults you trust?',
      'What would you do if something felt wrong?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give each child a card. Write three things your body does that you are thankful for.',
      'Write three trusted adults. Say the rule aloud together: “Say no, get away, tell.”',
      'Draw one thing you will do to care for your body this week.',
    ],
    close='Pray the guardian angel prayer together. Say, “You are God’s gift. Take care of yourself, and tell someone you trust if you need help.”'),
  tryit=['List three thanks for your body.', 'Write three trusted adults.', 'Say the rule: no, get away, tell.'],
  saintStory='Agnes was a young girl in Rome in the early 300s, when it was dangerous to be a Christian. Her name comes from the Latin word for lamb, and from a Greek word that means pure. She came from a wealthy family. She was about twelve or thirteen when the story of her death was first told.\nAccording to tradition, many young men wanted to marry her, but she said she belonged to Jesus. Some people reported her to the authorities as a Christian. The Roman officials tried to frighten her into giving up her faith. She did not. She was killed for being a Christian, around the year 304. The Church in Rome honored her very early, and her name is in the Roman Canon of the Mass.\nThe Church celebrates her on January 21. Every year, on her feast day, two lambs are blessed at the Basilica of Sant’Agnese in Rome. Their wool is used to make the palliums that the pope gives to new archbishops. She is the patron of young girls, and a sign of how God gives strength to the very young.',
),

L('Choosing well: conscience, saying sorry and the examination of the day',
  'Fifth graders are preparing for deeper moral thinking and often for the Sacrament of Penance. The Church’s teaching on conscience, examination, contrition and Confession gives them a simple daily practice: look back kindly, name what went well and what went wrong, say sorry, and start again. This lesson removes fear from the examination and shows it as a conversation with a God who is gentle.',
  '1776-1785,1451-1456,1454,1458,2559,1431-1433',
  'Psalm 139:1-4,23-24;Luke 15:11-24;1 John 1:8-9;Luke 15:3-7',
  'conscience contrition reconciliation penance',
  'At the end of a day, what do you think about? What would you like to think about?',
  'Learn the five-step “look back”: (1) Thank God for one good thing today. (2) Ask for light to see the day. (3) Look back: where did I love well? Where did I fail? (4) Say sorry for one thing and ask for help. (5) Look to tomorrow with one small plan. Do it once together, then once alone, and write one line for each step.',
  'evening-examen',
  id='5-L3',
  hook='Show a small mirror. Say, “A mirror helps us see our face. Our conscience helps us see our hearts.” Ask a child to hold it and say one good thing they notice in their classmates (not in the mirror). Then ask: “Why is it easier to look when we are kind to ourselves?”',
  teach=[
    'Your conscience is your inner sense of right and wrong. “Conscience is a judgment of reason by which the human person recognizes the moral quality of a concrete act” (CCC 1778). It speaks before we act (“Do this, avoid that”) and after (“You did well,” “You did wrong”). It needs training: by learning what is right, by praying, by asking wise people, and by looking back over our day. We always follow our conscience, but we must also form it (CCC 1783–1785).',
    'A great way to train it is to look back at the end of each day, kindly. The Church calls this an examination of conscience (CCC 1454). It is not a hunt for faults. It is like looking at a day with God, who loves you. Start with thanks, because most of the day is full of gifts. Then ask what went well and where you could have done better. Say sorry for what was wrong, and tell God you will try again. End by looking ahead to tomorrow.',
    'Being sorry is called contrition. “Contrition is sorrow of the soul and detestation for the sin committed, with the resolution not to sin again” (CCC 1451). It is more than feeling bad; it is deciding to turn back to God, the way the Prodigal Son did. And the father in that story did not wait for a perfect speech; he ran to him (Luke 15:20). God is more ready to forgive than we are to ask.',
    'The Sacrament of Penance, also called Reconciliation or Confession, is where God forgives us through a priest (CCC 1456, 1458). We tell our sins, the priest gives us a penance, and he says the words of absolution. The Church recommends that we go often, even if we have only small sins to confess, because it brings grace and helps us grow (CCC 1458). You do not need to be afraid. Priests have heard everything, and they keep everything secret. And remember: if you ever feel afraid of a grown-up or someone is hurting you, tell a trusted adult, and do not keep it a secret.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Why start the look back with thanks?',
    'What is the difference between feeling bad and being sorry?',
    'Why does the father in the story run to his son?',
    'What would help you feel brave about going to Confession?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What is conscience?|A feeling|A judgment of reason about whether an act is right or wrong|A rule book|Someone else’s opinion|1|CCC 1778.',
    'What is contrition?|Sorrow for sin with a decision not to sin again|Fear|Hiding|A gift|0|CCC 1451.',
    'What does the father do in the Prodigal Son?|Waits and scolds|Runs to meet his son and welcomes him|Sends him away|Ignores him|1|Luke 15:20.',
  ],
  life='Every night this week, do the five-step look back for two minutes. Write one line each night (a thank-you and a sorry). If you are ready, plan to go to Confession with your family this month.',
  saint='Francis de Sales: a gentle bishop who taught that we should be patient with ourselves, and that “nothing is so strong as gentleness.”',
  misc=[
    '“Examining my conscience means finding everything I did wrong.”',
    '“God is waiting to punish me.”',
    '“Confession is only for big sins.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:confession'],
  mem='“If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just and will forgive us our sins.” (1 John 1:9)',
  assess='Ask each child to say the five steps of the look back, define contrition, and say what the father did in the Prodigal Son story.',
  script=dict(
    open='Hold up a small mirror. Say, “A mirror helps us see our faces. Our conscience helps us see our hearts.” Ask, “How do you feel when someone looks at you kindly? How do you want to look at yourself?” Say, “Today we learn to look back on the day with God, kindly.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Conscience is your inner sense of right and wrong. It speaks before you act: do this, don’t do that. It also speaks after: you did well, or you did wrong. It needs training. We train it by learning what is right, by praying, by asking wise people, and by looking back over our day.”',
      'Say: “Here’s a way to look back. It’s called an examination of conscience. It is not a hunt for faults. It is looking at the day with God, who loves you. One: thank him for something good. Two: ask for light. Three: look back and see where you loved well and where you failed. Four: say sorry and ask for help. Five: look ahead to tomorrow with one small plan.”',
      'Say: “Being sorry is called contrition. It’s more than feeling bad. It is deciding to turn back to God. The Prodigal Son did that. His father didn’t wait for a perfect speech. He ran to him. God is more ready to forgive than we are to ask.”',
      'Say: “The Sacrament of Penance is where God forgives us through a priest. We tell our sins, the priest gives us a penance, and he says the words of absolution. The Church recommends going often, even for small sins, because it brings grace. Priests have heard everything and keep it secret. And if anyone ever hurts you or scares you, tell a trusted adult. That is never a secret to keep.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What is one good thing from your day to thank God for?',
      'What does the father in the story teach you about God?',
      'What would help you feel brave about Confession?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Teach the five steps on the board: Thank, Light, Look back, Sorry, Tomorrow.',
      'Do one together in silence for two minutes, with soft music.',
      'Each child writes one line for each step and keeps the card.',
    ],
    close='Pray the evening examen together briefly. Say, “Look back kindly. God is gentle with you.”'),
  tryit=['Learn the five steps of the look back.', 'Do one together in silence.', 'Write one line for each step.'],
  saintStory='Francis de Sales was born in 1567 in the Savoy, a region now in France. His father wanted him to be a lawyer, and sent him to study in Paris and Padua. As a young man he went through a terrible fear that he was damned, and he was almost sick with worry. Then one day in a church in Paris he prayed the Memorare and felt his fear lift. He decided to love God for love’s sake, and not out of fear.\nHe became a priest, and later bishop of Geneva, a city then full of tension between Catholics and Calvinists. He traveled on foot through the mountains, slipping pamphlets under doors. He was famous for his patience and gentleness, and many were converted.\nHe wrote the Introduction to the Devout Life, which teaches ordinary people how to grow in holiness in everyday life. He told his readers to be patient with themselves, to start again after a failure, and not to be anxious. He died in 1622 and was canonized in 1665. In 1877 he was named a Doctor of the Church, and he is the patron of writers and journalists.',
),
])
